WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.840
What happens when you paste the name of a classic&nbsp;
movie on a bad movie, and call the thing a sequel?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.960
Let’s say the results are less&nbsp;
than stellar in the case of “S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.960
Darko”. Stick around to see the full&nbsp;
list of lame cinematic follow-ups.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.400
Horror films and psychological thrillers&nbsp;
are gold mines for unnecessary add-ons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.720
and plenty of franchises — like "Hellraiser"&nbsp;
and "Child's Play," for instance — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.560
have spawned numerous sequels that&nbsp;
don't add much to the original story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.640
In many cases, these sequels end up going&nbsp;
direct-to-video — or straight to streaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.840
nowadays — which usually indicates that their&nbsp;
quality is substantially lower than the original.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.440
One of the most egregious examples of this is the&nbsp;
direct-to-video sequel to Mary Harron's "American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.400
Psycho," the classic slasher film starring&nbsp;
Christian Bale as serial killer Patrick Bateman.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.800
Released two years after the original,&nbsp;
"American Psycho II: All American Girl"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.640
stars Mila Kunis as a college student who becomes&nbsp;
a murderer during her time studying criminology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.240
Christian Bale isn't in the film, but&nbsp;
Patrick Bateman does appear — albeit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.680
played by someone else with his face&nbsp;
never shown. The connection to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.440
first film is small, but that's because&nbsp;
there was originally no connection at all.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.240
"American Psycho II" began as an original&nbsp;
screenplay called "The Girl Who Wouldn't Die," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.000
was undeservedly given the "American&nbsp;
Psycho" title late in production in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.480
a transparent bid to capitalize on the&nbsp;
original film's success. It was a cheap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:26.240
cash grab that definitely resulted in more&nbsp;
than a few disgruntled Blockbuster customers.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:27.920
"I have to return some videotapes."

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.280
"The Blair Witch Project" changed the horror&nbsp;
game when it arrived in the summer of 1999.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.000
One of the earliest examples of the modern&nbsp;
found-footage genre, the story of three film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.520
students lost in the woods was an unprecedented&nbsp;
success that spawned a sequel only a year later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.160
However, it wasn't originally supposed to&nbsp;
be released — at least not that quickly.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.400
After "The Blair Witch Project" broke the&nbsp;
record for the best budget-to-box-office ratio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.280
in American history, its distributor, Artisan&nbsp;
Entertainment, wasted no time in greenlighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.080
a second installment. While production&nbsp;
company Haxan Films didn't want to rush it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.320
Artisan pushed on regardless, choosing to&nbsp;
move forward without the involvement of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.320
either of the original "Blair Witch"&nbsp;
directors. True crime documentarian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.760
Joe Berlinger took the helm, but&nbsp;
even he was dissatisfied with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.160
final product after studio executives made&nbsp;
sweeping changes to his initial vision.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.320
The final product was "Book of Shadows:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.160
Blair Witch 2," which came and&nbsp;
went with little fanfare or praise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.960
The "Blair Witch" franchise was rebooted with&nbsp;
another installment in 2016, but the magic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:33.360
the first film still has yet to be replicated —&nbsp;
and it's hard to believe that it ever will be.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.880
"The Bourne Identity" kicked off the Bourne&nbsp;
franchise with a bang, telling the exciting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:45.360
yet thoughtful story of an amnesiac CIA operative&nbsp;
on a mission to discover his past. While not every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.760
film in the series was universally acclaimed,&nbsp;
the first three were positively received,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:55.360
bringing in a combined 950 million dollars at&nbsp;
the box office. While "The Bourne Identity"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.480
was an adaptation of the first installment of&nbsp;
Robert Ludlum's bestselling trilogy of novels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.760
that didn't mean that production on follow-up film&nbsp;
"The Bourne Supremacy" was a forgone conclusion.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.760
Matt Damon, the face of four out of&nbsp;
five of the films in the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:15.280
has commented that "The Bourne Identity" was never&nbsp;
made with a trilogy or even a sequel in mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.360
According to Damon, expectations were low&nbsp;
due to a number of production problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.920
along with the film's release date being&nbsp;
the same as the live-action "Scooby-Doo."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.280
Even though the Mystery Gang brought in&nbsp;
a bit more cash, "The Bourne Identity"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.840
defied expectations and revolutionized&nbsp;
the action genre for years to follow.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.520
Harold Ramis' "Caddyshack," the 1980 comedy about&nbsp;
the workers and members of an elite golf club,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.200
features an all-star cast that&nbsp;
includes Bill Murray and Chevy Chase.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.760
After the picture surpassed expectations and&nbsp;
catapulted Rodney Dangerfield to big-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.480
stardom, Warner Bros. requested a sequel…&nbsp;
and things got complicated from there.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:56.160
Production on "Caddyshack II" was a mess right&nbsp;
from the start. An initially apathetic Ramis only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.200
agreed to the sequel at Dangerfield's request,&nbsp;
but when numerous story ideas and rewrites left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.400
Dangerfield unsatisfied, he and Ramis dropped out&nbsp;
of the project a few months later. Chevy Chase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.440
was the only original cast member who would end up&nbsp;
appearing in the sequel, albeit in a smaller role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.360
The actors who replaced the original cast, as&nbsp;
well as their new characters, were the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:20.320
nails in the coffin for this troubled sequel. With&nbsp;
terrible reviews and dismal box office returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.720
"Caddyshack II" is a follow-up that reaffirms&nbsp;
that some ideas should just be one and done.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:32.240
Brian De Palma's 1976 adaptation of Stephen King's&nbsp;
"Carrie" is nothing less than a horror classic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.800
The tale of a high school outcast who wreaks&nbsp;
telekinetic havoc on her bullies has not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.760
been remade for film and television multiple times&nbsp;
over the years, but also inspired a sequel that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:47.120
was released a whole 23 years after the original.&nbsp;
Set within the same universe as the original film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.440
"The Rage: Carrie 2" centers around a young&nbsp;
woman named Rachel who has powers similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:57.040
to Carrie, and uses them to exact revenge on the&nbsp;
classmates who drove her only friend to suicide.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.600
Much like "American Psycho II," "The Rage:&nbsp;
Carrie 2" started as an original story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.560
but the powers that be thought that making Rachel&nbsp;
and Carrie related would boost ticket sales.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:10.320
Despite the return of Amy Irving&nbsp;
as Sue Snell, it didn't work:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:14.240
"The Rage: Carrie 2" was panned by&nbsp;
critics and failed to make a profit.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.560
Once a studio decides that it wants a&nbsp;
sequel, there isn't much that can stop it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.960
from happening — but it definitely doesn't help&nbsp;
if the people responsible for the original film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:27.360
want nothing to do with it. One filmmaker who&nbsp;
was particularly uninterested in returning under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:32.240
studio control was Richard Kelly, the filmmaker&nbsp;
behind the 2001 science fiction coming-of-age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:37.120
film "Donnie Darko." Although "Donnie Darko"&nbsp;
didn't do well financially on initial release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:41.280
it's since gained a cult following thanks to&nbsp;
its enigmatic plot that follows a troubled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.800
high schooler who's seeing visions&nbsp;
of a strange man in a rabbit suit.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.680
"I made a new friend." "Real&nbsp;
or imaginary?" "Imaginary."

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.600
In 2009, the sequel "S. Darko" was&nbsp;
released, centering around Donnie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.080
youngest sister and featuring a similarly&nbsp;
strange plot. According to Kelly, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.480
not only did he have no involvement in "S.&nbsp;
Darko," but he's never even seen the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.640
and has no plans to watch it any time soon.&nbsp;
That's probably for the best, considering that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.040
critics and moviegoers didn't seem to like it&nbsp;
either. Even though he hasn't released a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:15.760
since "The Box" in 2009, Kelly has promised&nbsp;
his own "Darko" follow-up in the future.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:23.440
William Friedkin's 1973 blockbuster "The Exorcist"&nbsp;
is widely regarded as one of the best horror films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.440
ever made, frightening in a way that few&nbsp;
movies at the time were able to match.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.000
The battle between two priests and the demon&nbsp;
possessing 12-year-old Regan spawned a franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.680
that includes several sequels, a television&nbsp;
series, and a reboot trilogy slated for 2023.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.280
William Peter Blatty, the author of the&nbsp;
novel the film is based on, produced the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:45.680
first film and wrote the script, winning the&nbsp;
Academy Award for Best Adapted Screenplay.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:50.080
Since "The Exorcist" was one of the biggest&nbsp;
films of the 1970s, Warner Bros. was hungry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:55.600
for a sequel, but neither Blatty nor Friedkin were&nbsp;
interested in signing on. Regardless, production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:59.680
on "Exorcist II: The Heretic" eventually&nbsp;
went ahead with a new writer, new producers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:04.160
a new director, and the return of Linda Blair's&nbsp;
Regan as a young teenager dealing with the trauma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:09.120
of the previous film's events. Whereas the first&nbsp;
film elicited scares, the sequel drew anger and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:14.160
laughter from both critics and moviegoers, failing&nbsp;
to meet financial expectations in the process.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:19.200
Blatty eventually returned to write and direct&nbsp;
"The Exorcist III," which was released 17 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.000
after the original and completely ignored the&nbsp;
events of "The Heretic." The threequel got much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:29.120
better reviews and has many more fans, but it's&nbsp;
still not as universally beloved as the original.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.320
Sequels typically arrive no more than a few years&nbsp;
after the films they're following up. The odds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.880
success tend to fall the longer it takes, but&nbsp;
a long wait can still end up with a big hit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.560
That wasn't the case, unfortunately,&nbsp;
for "Gremlins II: The New Batch."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:46.960
The sequel to 1984's "Gremlins" showed up&nbsp;
in theaters six years after the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:51.440
and despite earning positive reviews,&nbsp;
it had a dismal box office run.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.200
Director Joe Dante didn't want to make&nbsp;
a "Gremlins" sequel when Warner Bros.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:59.280
originally asked, but he eventually agreed on&nbsp;
the condition that he could have full creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:03.600
control. Dante's vision resulted in a film&nbsp;
that saw the Gremlins invade a New York City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.880
skyscraper, featuring several instances of&nbsp;
wildly anarchic meta humor. "The New Batch"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.320
didn't catch on during its initial release,&nbsp;
but it's acquired a cult following over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:15.530
the years — there's plenty of fans hoping&nbsp;
that they'll live to see a "Gremlins III."

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:20.960
It's one thing to not be interested in a sequel&nbsp;
— it's another to be forced into one thanks to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:26.960
contract. That's exactly what happened to Jan&nbsp;
de Bont, director of the runaway bus action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.520
film "Speed." De Bont felt that the story of&nbsp;
"Speed" had reached its natural conclusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:36.960
but the surprise success of the first film meant&nbsp;
he was contractually obligated to make one anyway.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.960
Thus, "Speed 2: Cruise Control" was&nbsp;
born. Replacing the bus with a boat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:46.240
and Keanu Reeves with Jason Patric, "Speed&nbsp;
2" failed to recapture the excitement of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:51.280
original despite featuring the always-entertaining&nbsp;
Willem Dafoe as the main villain. Even though it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.440
featured one of the biggest, most expensive&nbsp;
movie stunts of the 90s, "Cruise Control" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:59.600
only talked about nowadays when listing&nbsp;
the worst sequels in cinematic history.

